## Why This Is Asked

Tests for high-concurrency, real-time messaging with ordering and durability are common in collaboration apps. This question probes ability to design end-to-end tests that cover connection scalability, in-order delivery, offline replay, backpressure, and resilience to broker outages.

## Key Concepts

- WebSocket load testing and client choreography
- In-order delivery guarantees with per-room sequencing
- Durable stores for replay (Redis Streams, Kafka, or similar)
- Backpressure handling and reconnection logic
- Failure modes: partial broker outages, network partitions

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch for sequencing validation
let roomId = 'room-42';
let lastSeq = 0;
ws.onmessage = (evt) => {
  const msg = JSON.parse(evt.data);
  if (msg.roomId === roomId) {
    if (msg.seq <= lastSeq) fail('out-of-order');
    lastSeq = msg.seq;
  }
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure end-to-end latency under peak load and under broker outage?
- What tooling would you use to generate 10k concurrent connections and validate ordering?

## Why This Is Asked

End-to-end tests for asynchronous batch APIs ensure reliability where results arrive via webhooks and not immediately. It tests idempotency, security of callbacks, partial successes, and retry/backfill strategies under realistic traffic.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotent batch submission
- Webhook validation (signature)
- Eventual consistency and SLA
- Partial failures and per-record status transitions
- Replay/backfill of results

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: validate webhook signature during callback
const crypto = require('crypto');
function validWebhook(payload, sig, secret){
  const h = crypto.createHmac('sha256', secret).update(payload).digest('hex');
  return crypto.timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(sig), Buffer.from(h));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate bursty webhook delivery and ensure ordering guarantees?
- How would you test idempotency keys at scale with distributed queues?

## Why This Is Asked
